  "name": null,
  "problem": "At which values of $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3)$ does $f$ have a critical point?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The function $f$ has a critical point at $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
  "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{\u2032}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{\u2032}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nIn part 1, we obtained\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{7(5-4x)}{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7(5-4x)}{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 7(5-4x) & =0 \\\\ 5-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\n- Second, we find the points in the domain of $f(x)$ where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ does not exist. Factoring our expression for ${f}^{\u2032}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{7(5-4x)}{[(2x+1)(x-3)]^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe see that ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ does not exist at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=3.$ But these points do not lie in the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},3).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the only critical point of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$",
